var e=1,t=`ci-governance-01`,n=`corporate-issuers`,r=`Governance, Control and the Rights You Actually Have`,i=`Who decides, who benefits, and why a minority stake in a private company is worth reading the shareholders' agreement for.`,a=`core`,o=[`ci-allocation-01`],s=9,c=[`CFA-L1`,`CAIA-L1`,`practical`],l=[{type:`concept`,id:`what-it-is`,body:`[[corporate-governance|Corporate governance]] is the system determining who makes decisions in a company and on whose behalf. It exists because of a structural problem rather than a moral one.

The problem is the **[[agency-problem|agency problem]]**: the people running a company are usually not the people who own it. Their interests overlap and do not coincide. A manager wants scale, security and optionality; an owner wants return on capital. Neither is behaving badly — the incentives simply point in different directions, and governance is the machinery for narrowing the gap.

Three mechanisms do most of the work:

**The board.** Appointed to oversee management on shareholders' behalf, with the power to hire, fire and set pay.

**Incentives.** Pay linked to outcomes shareholders care about — which requires choosing the right measure, and the measure chosen shapes behaviour more than any policy statement.

**Rights.** Voting, information, veto and exit rights, defined by law and by contract.

**In public markets governance is largely a matter of law, disclosure and market pressure. In private markets it is a matter of contract — and the contract is negotiable, which means it is also readable.**`},{type:`concept`,id:`control-vs-ownership`,body:`**Ownership and control are separate things, and the gap between them is where minority investors get hurt.**

A 30% shareholder does not have 30% of the decisions. Depending on the documents, it may have almost none — or it may have a veto over everything that matters. The percentage is nearly uninformative on its own.

What determines actual influence:

**Board composition.** How many seats, and who appoints the chair. A board seat is worth more than several percent of equity.

**Reserved matters.** A list of decisions requiring the minority's consent — new debt, acquisitions, changes to the business plan, issuing new shares, related-party transactions.

**Anti-dilution protection.** Without it, the majority can issue new shares cheaply to itself and reduce the minority to insignificance.

**Information rights.** How often, in what detail, and with what audit standing. No information means no ability to exercise any other right.

**Exit rights.** [[tag-along|Tag-along]] lets you sell alongside the majority; [[drag-along|drag-along]] lets the majority force you to sell. One protects you, the other constrains you, and they usually appear together.

**So the question is never "how much do we own?" It is "what can happen without our consent?"** The answer is in the shareholders' agreement, and it is a shorter document than most people assume.`},{type:`intuition`,id:`incentives-are-the-policy`,body:`**Whatever a management team is paid on is what a management team will deliver. Everything else is a preference.**

This is worth taking literally, because the failures are so predictable that they can be read off the incentive plan in advance:

**Paid on EPS** — expect buybacks and accretive acquisitions, whatever the price.

**Paid on revenue growth** — expect volume at the expense of margin, and acquisitions.

**Paid on [[ebitda|EBITDA]]** — expect cost capitalisation, generous adjustments, and deferred maintenance.

**Paid on total shareholder return over three years** — expect actions that move the share price within three years, which is not the same as building durable value.

**Paid on ROIC or economic profit** — expect capital discipline, and expect the plan to be complained about as too complicated.

The last one is the closest alignment available and the least common, precisely because it is the hardest to hit by accident.

**In private markets the equivalent question is where the management equity sits and what it vests on.** A team with meaningful money in the deal on the same terms as the fund behaves differently from a team with an option package struck at a low reference price. And a team whose incentive pays out on an exit multiple rather than on cash returned will optimise for the appearance at the exit date — which is exactly when you are least able to check.`},{type:`example`,id:`worked-rights`,body:`Two funds each buy 30% of the same private company for the same price. Fund A negotiates a governance package; Fund B takes the standard documents.`,walkthrough:[`**Fund A** gets: two of five board seats, consent rights over new debt above a threshold, acquisitions, changes to the business plan and any share issuance, monthly management accounts with audited annual figures, tag-along rights, and pre-emption on new shares.`,`**Fund B** gets: one of five board seats, an annual report, and no consent rights.`,`Both own 30% of the equity. Now the majority proposes to raise 200 of debt to buy a related business from an entity it also owns.`,`**Fund A** can block it, or price its consent. **Fund B** can object at a board meeting and be outvoted.`,`**Read that carefully: identical economic ownership, identical price, and one investor has a veto over a related-party transaction while the other has a vote in a minute of a meeting.**`,`And the asymmetry compounds. Suppose the deal proceeds and performance deteriorates. Fund A sees monthly accounts and can act early. Fund B finds out in an annual report, by which point the debt is drawn and the asset is bought.`,`Sanity check worth reusing: **for any minority position, list what the majority can do without asking you.** If that list includes new debt, share issuance or related-party transactions, the percentage of equity you own is not the protection you think it is.`]},{type:`table`,id:`rights-table`,caption:`The rights worth negotiating, and what each prevents`,headers:[`Right`,`What it prevents`,`Cost of not having it`],rows:[[`Reserved matters consent`,`Debt, acquisitions and plan changes without you`,`The [[capital-structure|capital structure]] and strategy can change under you.`],[`Pre-emption / anti-dilution`,`Cheap share issuance to the majority`,`Your stake can be reduced without a sale.`],[`Information rights`,`Finding out late`,`Every other right becomes unusable — you cannot object to what you cannot see.`],[`[[tag-along|Tag-along]]`,`The majority selling and leaving you with a new controlling partner`,`You end up a minority alongside someone you did not choose.`],[`Board representation`,`Decisions taken without your view in the room`,`Influence limited to correspondence after the fact.`]]},{type:`concept`,id:`how-it-goes-wrong`,body:`**Related-party transactions.** Sales, purchases, leases or services between the company and entities the majority owns. Sometimes ordinary, always worth a list with amounts and counterparties, because it is the most direct route for value to leave a company without a decision anyone voted on.

**Dual-class shares.** Founders retaining voting control with a small economic stake. A legitimate structure with a specific consequence: the usual market discipline does not apply, so everything depends on the founder's judgement and intentions.

**Board independence in name only.** Directors described as independent who have long commercial relationships with the majority. Check tenure, other roles, and who proposed them.

**Weak audit arrangements.** In private companies the auditor's identity, scope and independence vary widely, and it determines how much weight the numbers can bear.

**Founder concentration.** A business where one person holds the customer relationships, the technical knowledge and the decisions is a governance risk as well as a key-person risk, and the two compound in exactly the same scenario.`},{type:`pitfall`,id:`gp-governance`,body:`**The governance you should examine most closely is your own manager's, and it is the one least often examined.**

A fund's [[limited-partnership-agreement|partnership agreement]] is a governance document. It determines what the [[general-partner|GP]] can do without asking the [[limited-partner|LPs]]: how far the strategy can drift, whether the fund can borrow, how assets are valued, what happens if key people leave, and how conflicts between funds are handled.

The conflicts worth naming specifically, because they are structural rather than hypothetical:

**Cross-fund transactions.** Selling an asset from one of the manager's funds to another. The manager sets the price and represents both sides.

**Continuation vehicles.** Moving an asset into a new vehicle with new investors. The manager advises the seller and the buyer and earns fees on both.

**Valuation.** The manager marks its own portfolio, and those marks drive reported performance, fundraising and sometimes fees.

**Key person departure.** Whether the LPs can stop the investment period if the people they backed leave.

**None of these is improper and all of them are conflicts, which is why the question is what process governs them rather than whether they exist.** An LP advisory committee with real consent rights over conflicted transactions is worth more than any assurance in a pitch.`},{type:`onTheJob`,id:`on-the-job`,body:`**For any minority stake, list what the majority can do without your consent.** That list is the position; the percentage is not.

**Ask for related-party transactions by counterparty and amount for three years.** Not as an accusation — as the standard route by which value leaves a company quietly.

**Read the management incentive plan and predict the behaviour from it.** Then check whether the last three years' decisions match. They usually do, which is the point.

**For a fund, ask what the LP advisory committee's consent rights actually cover.** Cross-fund transactions, continuation vehicles and valuation policy are the three that matter.

**Ask who values the portfolio, how often, and whether a third party is involved.** The manager marking its own homework is standard practice, and the process around it varies enormously.`},{type:`keyTakeaways`,id:`takeaways`,items:[`Governance exists because managers and owners have overlapping but different interests — a structural problem, not a moral one.`,`Ownership and control are separate. Two funds with identical 30% stakes had a veto over a related-party transaction and a vote in a minute respectively, depending only on the documents.`,`Whatever a team is paid on is what they will deliver: EPS pay predicts buybacks, EBITDA pay predicts add-backs, ROIC pay predicts capital discipline.`,`Examine your own manager's governance hardest — cross-fund transactions, continuation vehicles and self-valuation are structural conflicts, so ask what process governs them.`]}],u=[{id:`ci-governance-01-q1`,type:`mcq`,difficulty:2,tags:[`governance`,`agency`],concept:`what-it-is`,stem:`What is the agency problem in corporate governance?`,choices:[`Managers routinely act dishonestly toward shareholders`,`The people running a company are not the people who own it, so their interests overlap without coinciding`,`Shareholders have too much control over daily operations`,`Boards are legally prevented from removing management`],answerIndex:1,rationales:[`Dishonesty is a separate and much rarer problem. The agency problem operates through entirely honest people with different incentives.`,`Correct. A manager rationally values scale, security and optionality; an owner values return on capital. Governance is the machinery for narrowing that gap.`,`The usual complaint runs the other way, and shareholders are generally too dispersed to exercise close oversight.`,`Boards can and do remove management; that power is one of the main mechanisms.`],explanation:`Framing it as structural rather than moral is what makes the solutions obvious: change the incentives, add constraints, or concentrate ownership. All three are what a private equity model does, which is why the argument for it is a governance argument rather than a financing one.`},{id:`ci-governance-01-q2`,type:`mcq`,difficulty:3,tags:[`governance`,`minority-rights`],concept:`control-vs-ownership`,stem:`Two investors each own 30% of a private company. What most determines their actual influence?`,choices:[`The size of their stake, which is identical`,`The documents — board seats, reserved matters, information rights and anti-dilution protection`,`Which of them invested first`,`The company's dividend policy`],answerIndex:1,rationales:[`The stake is identical and the influence need not be, which is exactly the point.`,`Correct. Consent rights over debt, acquisitions and share issuance are what convert a shareholding into influence. Without them, a minority has a vote and little else.`,`Timing may explain who negotiated better terms and confers no rights by itself.`,`Dividend policy is one of the things influence is used to affect, not a source of it.`],explanation:`The practical version is a one-page list: what can the majority do without asking us? Producing that list from the shareholders' agreement takes an hour and is the single most useful piece of work available on a minority investment.`},{id:`ci-governance-01-q3`,type:`mcq`,difficulty:3,tags:[`governance`,`incentives`],concept:`incentives-are-the-policy`,stem:`A management team is paid on EBITDA growth. Which behaviour should you expect?`,choices:[`Disciplined capital allocation and higher returns on invested capital`,`Generous EBITDA adjustments, cost capitalisation and deferred maintenance spending`,`Higher dividends`,`Reduced leverage`],answerIndex:1,rationales:[`Capital discipline is what ROIC-based pay produces. EBITDA pay is indifferent to how much capital was used to grow it.`,`Correct. Every one of those raises EBITDA without improving the business, and all three are available to an honest team under pressure to hit a number.`,`Distributions are unrelated to an EBITDA target and reduce the cash available to grow it.`,`Leverage is invisible above the EBITDA line, so the incentive gives no reason to reduce it.`],explanation:`This is why reading the incentive plan before the strategy document is efficient: the plan predicts the strategy. And where the two disagree, the plan wins — which is a reliable enough rule to treat as a starting assumption.`},{id:`ci-governance-01-q4`,type:`tfj`,difficulty:4,tags:[`governance`,`misconception`],concept:`control-vs-ownership`,stem:`"We own 30%, so we can block anything significant." True or false — and why?`,isTrue:!1,justifications:[`False — a blocking right comes from the documents, not from the percentage; without reserved matters consent, 30% is a vote that can be outvoted`,`False — blocking requires at least 50% ownership in all cases`,`True — 30% is above the usual blocking threshold`,`False — because minority shareholders have no rights at all`],justificationIndex:0,rationales:[`Correct. Some decisions require supermajorities by law, and most operational and financing decisions do not. What a minority can block is what its contract says it can block.`,`Over-correction. Negotiated consent rights give small holders vetoes over specific matters all the time.`,`There is no universal blocking threshold at 30%; thresholds depend on jurisdiction and on the articles.`,`They have statutory protections and whatever the agreement gives them — the claim fails on the source of the right, not its existence.`],explanation:`The honest question at the negotiating stage is which three or four matters you most need consent over — usually new debt, share issuance and related-party transactions. Asking for everything gets refused; asking for those three is normal and is where the protection actually lies.`},{id:`ci-governance-01-q5`,type:`mcq`,difficulty:4,tags:[`governance`,`gp-conflicts`,`on-the-job`],concept:`gp-governance`,stem:`A manager proposes selling an asset from Fund III to a continuation vehicle it will also manage. What is the structural issue?`,choices:[`Continuation vehicles are not permitted under most partnership agreements`,`The manager advises both the seller and the buyer, sets the price, and earns fees on both sides`,`The asset must be independently revalued by law`,`Existing investors are always forced to sell`],answerIndex:1,rationales:[`They are widespread and generally permitted, which is why the process around them matters so much.`,`Correct. It is a genuine conflict rather than an impropriety: the same party represents both sides of a price negotiation and is paid on the outcome either way.`,`Independent valuation is good practice and not universally required, and its absence is one of the things to check.`,`Investors are typically offered a choice to roll or exit — the quality of the information behind that choice is the real question.`],explanation:`The questions that make it defensible: who set the price and on what evidence, was the market tested, did the LP advisory committee consent, and what did investors who rolled receive that those who exited did not. A manager with good answers usually offers them unprompted.`},{id:`ci-governance-01-q6`,type:`mcq`,difficulty:4,tags:[`governance`,`diligence`,`on-the-job`],concept:`how-it-goes-wrong`,stem:`Which single disclosure request is most efficient at finding value leaking out of a private company?`,choices:[`The management incentive plan`,`Related-party transactions by counterparty and amount over three years`,`The audited financial statements`,`The board meeting minutes`],answerIndex:1,rationales:[`Highly informative about future behaviour, and it does not show what has already left.`,`Correct. Sales, purchases, leases and services with entities the majority owns are the most direct route for value to move out without any decision being voted on.`,`The statements aggregate; related-party detail sits in a note that is often brief.`,`Minutes record decisions taken, usually after the arrangement is in place.`],explanation:`Ask for it as a routine schedule rather than as a challenge, because most related-party dealing is legitimate — a founder's property company leasing a warehouse at market rent. What you are testing is whether the terms are market and whether the arrangement survives a change of ownership.`}],d=!1,f=null,p={schemaVersion:1,id:t,domain:n,title:r,summary:i,level:a,prereqs:o,estMinutes:9,examRelevance:c,lesson:l,questions:u,needsReview:!1,reviewNote:null};export{p as default,n as domain,s as estMinutes,c as examRelevance,t as id,l as lesson,a as level,d as needsReview,o as prereqs,u as questions,f as reviewNote,e as schemaVersion,i as summary,r as title};
